CAT = "rsracMaintain"; SEG = "MNT"
Q = [
("What does an acid test kit on a compressor oil sample check for?",
 "Oil breakdown", ["Oil viscosity grade", "Refrigerant type in use", "Oil level in the sump"],
 "Acid forms when moisture, heat or a burnout breaks down oil and refrigerant. A positive test calls for acid-removal driers and cleanup before damage spreads."),
("Why are megohmmeter readings of a large motor recorded at each scheduled visit?",
 "A falling trend warns of failure", ["To set the motor's speed", "To calibrate the overload", "Required to restart the motor"],
 "Insulation resistance that drops visit after visit shows winding insulation ageing or moisture, so the motor can be planned for repair before it grounds out."),
("An infrared camera scan of a disconnect shows one fuse clip much hotter than the others. What does it suggest?",
 "Loose connection", ["Normal load sharing", "Overcharged refrigerant", "Low supply voltage"],
 "A high-resistance connection heats under load. Thermal scans find it so it can be cleaned and tightened before it fails or starts a fire."),
("A run capacitor rated 45 microfarads measures 36 microfarads. What should be done?",
 "Replace it", ["Leave it in service", "Add a start capacitor", "Raise the supply voltage"],
 "36 is 20% below the rating, far outside the usual tolerance of about plus or minus 6%. A weak run capacitor makes the motor run hot and draw extra current."),
("Why are condenser coils on a restaurant's rooftop unit cleaned more often than an office's?",
 "Grease and lint foul them much faster", ["They run at a higher voltage", "They use a different refrigerant", "Codes require monthly cleaning"],
 "Kitchen exhaust grease and lint stick to nearby coils and trap dirt. Scheduled maintenance is set by the conditions, not a single fixed interval."),
("What does eddy current testing of a chiller's tubes find?",
 "Tube wall loss", ["Refrigerant charge level", "Oil acid content", "Bearing temperature"],
 "An eddy current probe pulled through each tube detects wall loss, pits and cracks, so failing tubes can be plugged or replaced before they leak water into the refrigerant."),
("Why is the condensate pan and drain of an air handler cleaned during maintenance?",
 "Slime can block the drain and overflow", ["To raise coil pressure", "To lower supply temperature", "To charge the system"],
 "Biological growth builds in damp pans and drains. A blocked drain overflows into the building and stagnant water can affect air quality."),
("Contactor contacts on a compressor are badly pitted. What is the risk if they are left?",
 "Heat and voltage drop", ["Higher suction pressure", "Refrigerant migration", "Lower oil temperature"],
 "Pitted contacts have high resistance, so they heat up and drop voltage to the compressor, and can weld shut. They are replaced, not filed, in most service practice."),
("Why is belt tension checked on a belt-driven blower at each service?",
 "Slipping cuts fan speed", ["Belts store refrigerant", "Tension sets the voltage", "Tight belts cool the motor"],
 "A slipping belt cuts fan speed and airflow and wears quickly; an overtight belt loads the bearings. Tension and alignment are set to the maker's values."),
("Vibration readings on a large compressor rise steadily over several months. What is the likely meaning?",
 "Developing mechanical wear", ["Better lubrication", "Lower refrigerant charge only", "Normal ageing of the paint"],
 "Trended vibration is a predictive maintenance tool. A rising level points to bearing wear, misalignment or loose mounts before a breakdown."),
("Why is a refrigerant leak check part of the scheduled maintenance on a large commercial system?",
 "Halocarbon rules require it", ["It replaces the oil change", "It resets the charge level", "It cleans the coils too"],
 "Halocarbon rules call for regular leak tests. The Federal Halocarbon Regulations, which cover federal systems, require them on systems over 10 kg at least once every calendar year and no more than 15 months apart, and provincial rules set leak checks for other systems."),
("A heat recovery ventilator's core is cleaned at maintenance. Why?",
 "Dust cuts heat transfer through it", ["To refill its refrigerant", "To reset its defrost timer", "To lower the fan voltage"],
 "Dust on the core plates blocks air passages and insulates the surfaces, reducing both ventilation and recovered heat. Filters and core are cleaned to the maker's schedule."),
("What is checked on an ice machine during routine maintenance?",
 "Scale on the evaporator", ["Supply voltage phase", "Condenser paint colour", "Room thermostat setting"],
 "Mineral scale on the freezing surface slows harvest and makes poor ice. Descaling and sanitizing with the maker's approved chemicals is routine service."),
("Why is the operating log of a chiller reviewed at each maintenance visit?",
 "Trends show slow problems early", ["It sets the next refrigerant price", "It replaces gauge readings", "It is needed to start the chiller"],
 "Daily readings of pressures, temperatures and approach show gradual changes, such as fouling or charge loss, that a single visit would miss."),
("Why are oil filters and strainers on a large screw compressor replaced on schedule?",
 "Clogging starves the bearings", ["They raise the refrigerant charge", "They control the slide valve", "They stop refrigerant leaks"],
 "Oil carries heat and lubricates the rotors and bearings. A blocked filter raises pressure drop and can trip the oil pressure safety or wear the bearings."),
("After maintenance changes the setpoints of an integrated system, what should be done?",
 "Record the new settings", ["Remove the old labels", "Reset the controller to defaults", "Leave settings unrecorded"],
 "Optimizing performance includes documenting what was changed, so the owner and the next mechanic know the current settings and why."),
]
